window.DDIA_MANIFEST = {"ch1":{"sections":[{"id":"data-systems","title":"Your application is now a data system"},{"id":"reliability","title":"Faults are inevitable; failures are optional"},{"id":"fault-types","title":"Three kinds of faults, and why redundancy fixes only one"},{"id":"describing-load","title":"Scalability starts with the right load parameter"},{"id":"describing-performance","title":"Response time is a distribution, not a number"},{"id":"queueing","title":"Why the queue, not the work, makes requests slow"},{"id":"tail-latency","title":"Why the slowest call wins"},{"id":"coping-with-load","title":"Scaling up, scaling out, and rethinking every 10x"},{"id":"operability","title":"Most of the cost comes after launch"},{"id":"simplicity-evolvability","title":"Fight accidental complexity so the system can evolve"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch1/fault-lab","title":"Fault lab: when faults become failures","caption":"Try Kill a server a few times, then Leap-second bug with the watchdog off and on, then Push bad config with All at once versus Canary plus auto-rollback. Notice that lowering the load (more spare servers) absorbs dead machines but does nothing against the bad config, and nothing against the leap-second bug either until a reboot brings servers back.","section":"fault-types"},{"id":"ch1/twitter-fanout","title":"Twitter timelines: fan-out on read vs write","caption":"Try Bob opens timeline under Pull, then Alice tweets and Celebrity tweets under Push, then the same buttons under Hybrid; drag the sliders to change the load. Notice the celebrity's delivery bar blowing past the 5-second goal under Push, while Hybrid turns that flood into one write plus a few lookups at read time.","section":"describing-load"},{"id":"ch1/percentiles","title":"Mean vs percentiles","caption":"Try adding a 30 s timeout or two, then toggling TCP retransmits and cache misses, then switching to Two servers. Notice that one timeout shifts the mean while the median barely moves, that each source of slowness moves a different percentile, and that the average of the two servers' p99s lands well below the true p99.","section":"describing-performance"},{"id":"ch1/queueing","title":"Queueing and the lying benchmark","caption":"Try the <em>Busy server</em> preset, then <em>Server-side timer</em>, then <em>Benchmark lies</em>. Notice that client p99 explodes as utilization nears 100% while the server-side p99 stays near 12 ms, and that the closed-loop benchmark records just one slow sample during the 1-second freeze; the correction toggle recovers most of what it missed.","section":"queueing"},{"id":"ch1/tail-latency","title":"Tail latency amplification","caption":"Try dragging fan-out to 100 with 1% slow calls, then switch on <em>Hedge slow calls</em>. Notice that 63% of page loads are slow without hedging, and only about 1% with it, for roughly 1% extra backend load. That rests on the model stated under the controls: slowness independent across replicas, and a hedge that fires just above normal latency.","section":"tail-latency"},{"id":"ch1/elastic-scaling","title":"Fixed fleet vs auto-scaling","caption":"Try <em>Flash sale</em> with the manual fleet, then switch to <em>Elastic</em>, then turn on <em>Pre-scale for the 12:00 sale</em>. Notice that the fixed fleet pays for the peak all day yet still fails during the sale, auto-scaling saves server-hours but lags the jump by its scale-up delay, and only planning ahead handles a known spike cleanly. (Synthetic model; units are servers of load.)","section":"coping-with-load"}],"quizzes":10,"words":15767,"minutes":72},"ch2":{"sections":[{"id":"why-data-models","title":"Every data model is a bet on which questions will be cheap"},{"id":"impedance-mismatch","title":"One résumé, four tables: the object-relational mismatch"},{"id":"many-to-one","title":"IDs, not strings: why many-to-one pulls you back to joins"},{"id":"repeating-history","title":"Document databases are repeating history, and history has a lesson"},{"id":"schema-flexibility","title":"Schemaless isn't schema-free: it's a question of where the schema lives"},{"id":"locality-and-convergence","title":"Locality, simpler code, and why the two models are converging"},{"id":"declarative-queries","title":"Say what, not how: why declarative queries won"},{"id":"mapreduce-querying","title":"MapReduce: a powerful runtime, an awkward query language"},{"id":"property-graphs","title":"When everything connects: property graphs and variable-depth queries"},{"id":"triples-and-datalog","title":"Graphs as facts: triple-stores, SPARQL and Datalog"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch2/normalize-or-embed","title":"Embed or reference?","caption":"Pick an operation and press <b>▶ Run</b>: <b>Load Ana's profile</b>, then <b>Rename Acme</b> with <b>Crash mid-write</b> switched on, then <b>Who worked at Acme?</b>; repeat with <b>Reference ids</b>. Notice that embedding wins the load (1 read against 5 joined rows), but the crash leaves two profiles still saying \"Acme Corp\" (and the reverse query, searching for the new name, silently skips them), while the relational row and the reference design rename with one write that can't be half-done.","section":"many-to-one"},{"id":"ch2/schema-on-read","title":"Where does the schema live?","caption":"Try <b>Ship v2</b>, then <b>+ Write user</b>, then <b>▶ Run reader</b> with <b>Reader handles old {name} docs</b> off, then on; finish with <b>+ Buggy write (typo)</b> and run the reader again. Notice that the table pays up front (a migration rewrites every row) and rejects the typo at the writer, while the document store accepts every shape and its errors surface later, in the reader.","section":"schema-flexibility"},{"id":"ch2/query-planner","title":"Say what, not how","caption":"Try running both programs, then turn on the index or pick 4 cores and run them again. Next, turn on <em>Hand-optimize the loop</em>, run it, press <em>Reorganize storage</em> and run it once more. Notice that the SQL text never changes while its plan does, and that after the reorganization the early-exit loop silently returns 2 of 6 sharks.","section":"declarative-queries"},{"id":"ch2/mapreduce-purity","title":"Why map and reduce must be pure","caption":"Try pressing Play with the pure mapper and shard B's crash turned on, then switch <em>Mapper</em> to <em>Impure</em> and play again. Notice that the reduce output is 10 · 9 · 6 both times, but the side-effect counter ends at 31 instead of 25: the dead attempt's pairs were discarded, its side effect couldn't be.","section":"mapreduce-querying"},{"id":"ch2/graph-traversal","title":"Born in the US, living in Europe","caption":"Pick a query and press <em>▶ Run query</em>: <em>Cypher *0..</em> first, then <em>SQL, fixed joins</em> with k from 1 up to 3, then turn on <em>Add a level: Greater London</em> and run k = 3 again. Notice that the fixed-join query never raises an error; it just finds fewer people, while Cypher and <em>SQL WITH RECURSIVE</em> find Lucy, Sam and Maya at any depth.","section":"property-graphs"},{"id":"ch2/datalog-fixpoint","title":"Datalog rules reach a fixpoint","caption":"Try pressing Play and watching the staircase: round 1 gives each place its own name, every later round climbs one level, and round 5 derives nothing. Then turn on <em>Add a level: Ada County</em> and play again. Notice that it simply takes one more round; nothing in the rules mentions depth.","section":"triples-and-datalog"}],"quizzes":10,"words":16906,"minutes":77},"ch3":{"sections":[{"id":"log-and-hash-index","title":"A two-line database, and the index that rescues its reads"},{"id":"sstables-and-lsm-trees","title":"Sort the segments: SSTables and the LSM-tree"},{"id":"bloom-filters-and-compaction","title":"Keeping LSM reads fast: Bloom filters and compaction strategies"},{"id":"b-trees","title":"B-trees: a shallow tree of fixed-size pages"},{"id":"write-ahead-log","title":"Crash in the middle of a page split: the write-ahead log"},{"id":"lsm-vs-btree","title":"LSM vs B-tree: choose which amplification you pay"},{"id":"other-indexes","title":"Secondary indexes, many dimensions, and keeping it all in memory"},{"id":"oltp-vs-olap","title":"Two workloads, opposite needs: OLTP, analytics and the data warehouse"},{"id":"column-storage","title":"Read only what the query touches: column storage and compression"},{"id":"column-writes-and-cubes","title":"Writing to a column store, and precomputing the answers"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch3/log-hash-index","title":"Append-only log with a hash index","caption":"Try Write on one key a few times, then Read and Read without index, then Compact and merge. Finally press Crash and restart with Hint files off, then on. Notice that an indexed read costs one disk read however many stale records pile up (none for a missing key), that garbage stays on disk until compaction, and that after a crash the RAM index must be rebuilt (slowly by scanning, quickly from hint files) while the torn last record fails its checksum and is dropped.","section":"log-and-hash-index"},{"id":"ch3/lsm-tree","title":"LSM-tree write and read paths","caption":"Try Get with key e first: a tombstone in SST 3 hides the older e:1 in L1. Then press Put x8 random and watch the memtable flush and L0 compact, and press Scan c–f to see a range read merge every file. Switch Auto-compact off and keep writing until writes stall: Flush is blocked too, and only Compact clears the stall. Finally switch the WAL off, make a few Puts and press Crash. Notice that Last read and Space amp climb while compaction is paused, Write amp climbs when it runs, and only the writes made without the WAL are lost. (Toy thresholds: compaction at 4 L0 files, a stall at 6; RocksDB's defaults are 4, 20 and 36.)","section":"sstables-and-lsm-trees"},{"id":"ch3/bloom-filter","title":"Bloom filter: definitely not, or maybe","caption":"Try querying words that aren't in the set until you hit a false positive, then press Query all keys and compare the observed rate with the predicted one. Insert more keys or drag Bits (m) down to watch the filter saturate, compare k with the Best k stat, and press Try to delete. Notice that an inserted word always answers maybe, and that clearing a key's bits would also clear bits another key needs.","section":"bloom-filters-and-compaction"},{"id":"ch3/btree","title":"B-tree lookups and page splits","caption":"Try Insert with the default key 75, which fits, then set the key to 47 and insert again: its leaf is full, so it splits, the root overflows too, and a new root appears on top. Then try Lookup and Range scan 20–60. Notice that every leaf stays at the same depth, that one insert can write several pages (the Write cost stat), and in the At real scale panel how four levels of 500-way pages reach 256 TB.","section":"b-trees"},{"id":"ch3/wal-crash","title":"Crash mid-split: with and without a WAL","caption":"Try Play with the write-ahead log Off: the power fails after the first page write of a split, and key 50, committed long before, becomes unreachable. Switch Power fails after to 2 pages written to see an orphan page. Then turn the log On and play again. Notice that the client hears OK only once the log record is durable, and that recovery redoes only the pages whose LSN is older than the record. (The viz logs one record for the whole split; real engines log each page change plus a commit record.)","section":"write-ahead-log"},{"id":"ch3/column-store","title":"Row vs column storage","caption":"Try Q1 and compare how many cells each layout reads (60 vs 24), then switch the sort to \"product, date\" and watch each product bitmap's run-length encoding shrink to two numbers. Pick Q2 to see a WHERE clause become a bitwise AND, and Q3 to see the one case where the row store wins. Finally, choose \"product column only\": compression looks perfect, but the answers turn red, because the columns no longer line up.","section":"column-storage"}],"quizzes":10,"words":17867,"minutes":81},"ch4":{"sections":[{"id":"why-encoding","title":"Data outlives the code that wrote it"},{"id":"language-specific-formats","title":"Built-in serialization: one line of code, years of regret"},{"id":"textual-formats","title":"JSON, XML and CSV: universal, readable and subtly lossy"},{"id":"thrift-protobuf","title":"Schemas let you stop shipping field names"},{"id":"field-tags-evolution","title":"Field tags are forever"},{"id":"avro","title":"Avro: no tags, two schemas, matched by name"},{"id":"merits-of-schemas","title":"Schemas are documentation that can't go stale"},{"id":"dataflow-databases","title":"Through databases: a message to your future self"},{"id":"dataflow-services","title":"Through services: REST, RPC and clients you can't upgrade"},{"id":"dataflow-messages","title":"Through messages: brokers and actors decouple sender from receiver"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch4/json-numbers","title":"JSON numbers vs 64-bit IDs","caption":"Try the presets, then pick \"Largest safe: 2⁵³ − 1\" and press +1 twice. Drag the ID-size slider toward 63 bits, and switch \"Also send id_str\" off and on again. Notice that past 2⁵³ two different IDs decode to the same double, that the tweet-style ID comes back with wrong trailing digits even though it is stored exactly, and that only the string copy survives every case.","section":"textual-formats"},{"id":"ch4/encoding-bytes","title":"One record, six encodings","caption":"Try each format and tap single bytes to see what they mean. Then set favoriteNumber to -1, then to absent, and drag the interests slider. Notice that the pink field-name bytes disappear as soon as there is a schema, that Protobuf spends 10 bytes on -1 where Thrift Compact and Avro spend one, and that an unset optional field costs nothing in Protobuf and Thrift but still one byte in Avro.","section":"thrift-protobuf"},{"id":"ch4/tag-evolution","title":"Evolving a Protobuf schema","caption":"Try every entry in \"Schema change\" and press Decode to watch the parser walk the chunks. Notice which changes fail loudly (adding or deleting a required field), which corrupt data with no error at all (reusing or renumbering a tag, widening int32 to int64 when old code reads big values), and that old code skips an unknown tag using nothing but its wire type.","section":"field-tags-evolution"},{"id":"ch4/avro-resolution","title":"Writer's schema meets reader's schema","caption":"Try 'Reader adds a field without a default', then press Swap writer and reader; do the same with 'Rename with an alias' and 'Promote int to long'. Notice that the same pair of schemas fails in one direction and works in the other, and that the byte strip always follows the writer's order, even when the reader's fields are shuffled. Switch where the writer's schema travels to see what each record pays to identify it.","section":"avro"},{"id":"ch4/rolling-upgrade","title":"Rolling upgrade on a shared database","caption":"Try Play the story with 'Drop (model object)', then again with 'Preserve' and 'Reject'. Notice that under Drop no request fails (Failed requests stays at 0) yet Fields lost ticks up, while under Reject nothing is lost but Failed requests climbs instead. Then upgrade a node, set the photo, roll the node back and edit the bio again: a rollback is old code meeting new data.","section":"dataflow-databases"},{"id":"ch4/api-evolution","title":"Old clients vs a changing API","caption":"Try the default change (a new response field): it's additive, yet the partner's Jackson client breaks under every strategy. Then pick 'Rename amount to amount_cents' and switch between In place, /v2 URL and Pinned dates, and finish with 'Add enum value status: pending'. Notice that versioning rescues renames, type changes and removals, but can't save clients from additions they refuse to tolerate, and that the 2019 script misreads 'pending' without raising any error.","section":"dataflow-services"}],"quizzes":10,"words":17381,"minutes":79},"ch5":{"sections":[{"id":"why-replicate","title":"Three reasons to copy your data, and the one hard part"},{"id":"leaders-and-followers","title":"One leader, many followers: who waits for whom"},{"id":"node-outages","title":"Adding followers and surviving crashes: why failover is the dangerous part"},{"id":"replication-logs","title":"What actually travels from leader to follower"},{"id":"replication-lag","title":"When followers fall behind: three anomalies with names"},{"id":"multi-leader","title":"More than one leader: when a single write location isn't enough"},{"id":"write-conflicts","title":"Write conflicts: avoid them, or make every replica pick the same winner"},{"id":"leaderless-quorums","title":"No leader at all: quorums instead of failover"},{"id":"quorum-limits","title":"The fine print: when w + r > n still returns stale data"},{"id":"concurrent-writes","title":"Concurrent writes: track causality, not clocks"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch5/sync-async","title":"Sync, semi-sync and async replication","caption":"Try Async: press Write twice, and as soon as both new squares turn green, press Crash leader. Notice the red squares on the old leader: writes the client was told succeeded, gone after failover. Reset, pick Sync and Crash F2: the next Write blocks until you revive F2. In Semi-sync, Crash F1 and watch F2 take over as the synchronous follower.","section":"leaders-and-followers"},{"id":"ch5/failover","title":"When failover goes wrong","caption":"Pick a story and press Play. Run Lost writes and Split brain twice, with the toggle off and then on (Reused IDs has no defence to switch on). Notice that in Lost writes the new leader reuses #42 for a different write, that in Reused IDs nothing inside the database looks wrong, and that in Split brain the Leaders counter reaches 2 unless fencing is on.","section":"node-outages"},{"id":"ch5/replication-lag","title":"Replication lag anomalies","caption":"Try Read your writes with Fix off and drag the lag across 1.0 s: the outcome flips at exactly that point. Then try each fix. Notice that every fix is a rule about where a read may go, and that in Monotonic reads the pinned user reads from the slow follower: stale, but never backwards.","section":"replication-lag"},{"id":"ch5/multi-leader-conflict","title":"Multi-leader write conflicts","caption":"Try <strong>Both edit at once</strong> with 'Apply in arrival order', then with each of the other rules. Notice that only the naive rule leaves the datacenters disagreeing, that last write wins and replica ID quietly discard an edit someone was told was saved, and that <strong>Single leader (EU)</strong> removes the conflict but makes Bob's write slow, or impossible once you turn on <strong>Link down</strong>.","section":"write-conflicts"},{"id":"ch5/quorum","title":"Quorums, read repair and sloppy quorums","caption":"Try <strong>Read</strong> with the defaults: it is always fresh, and read repair brings R3 up to date. Then set w and r to 1, press <strong>Write</strong>, and press <strong>Read</strong> as soon as the write is acknowledged: the worst-case read hits a replica the slow copies have not reached yet. Back at w = 2, tap two of the three replicas to take them down and write: the client gets an error, yet the surviving replica keeps the value. Notice that read repair only fixes replicas that answered, while <strong>Run anti-entropy</strong> fixes them all. (The <strong>Sloppy quorum</strong> toggle is the subject of the next section.)","section":"leaderless-quorums"},{"id":"ch5/concurrent-writes","title":"Concurrent writes: LWW, siblings and CRDTs","caption":"Try <strong>Play: the book's cart</strong> in Siblings mode, then switch <strong>Server keeps</strong> to LWW and play it again. Then run <strong>a removal comes back</strong> and <strong>remove vs re-add</strong> under Siblings, Siblings + tombstones and OR-Set CRDT. Notice that LWW loses eggs and ham, plain siblings bring a removed item back, item tombstones wipe out an independent re-add, and only the OR-Set gets both right.","section":"concurrent-writes"}],"quizzes":10,"words":20092,"minutes":91},"ch6":{"sections":[{"id":"why-partition","title":"Why one machine eventually stops being enough"},{"id":"key-range","title":"Sorted key ranges: cheap scans, dangerous hot spots"},{"id":"hash-partitioning","title":"Hashing buys evenness by giving up order"},{"id":"hot-spots","title":"When one key is hotter than a whole partition"},{"id":"secondary-indexes","title":"Secondary indexes: pay on writes or pay on reads"},{"id":"rebalancing","title":"Rebalancing without moving everything"},{"id":"rebalancing-strategies","title":"Three ways to decide what moves"},{"id":"rebalancing-operations","title":"Who presses the rebalance button?"},{"id":"request-routing","title":"Finding the right partition when it keeps moving"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch6/range-vs-hash","title":"Range vs hash partitioning","caption":"Try the opening scene first: under <em>Time range</em> (four 6-hour ranges, as in the opening story), every write for today's 14:xx lands in P2 on node C, the story's overloaded third node. Drag the Clock and the hot spot moves with it. Switch to <em>Hash of key</em>: the bars even out, but <em>Query: S3, last 2 h</em> now asks all four partitions. Then pick <em>Sensor, then time</em> and run both queries. Notice that the per-sensor query becomes a single scan, while the all-sensors query needs eight. Finally turn on <em>S3 is 10x busier</em>: a key prefix spreads load only when traffic is spread evenly across its values.","section":"hash-partitioning"},{"id":"ch6/hot-key-split","title":"Splitting a celebrity key","caption":"Try the opening scene: all 2,500 writes/s for post:42 carry one key, so P3 runs at 300% of its limit while the other seven idle at 50%. Raise <em>Split into k sub-keys</em> to 5, then 8, pressing <em>Read like count</em> each time, then try 9 and 16. Notice that 8 sub-keys already spread the writes as far as they can go: at 9, P3 holds two sub-keys and is over its limit again, and 16 looks just like 8, while every read keeps paying k requests. Then set k back to 1, push the hot key to 6,000/s and turn on <em>Combine writes in app</em>: the hot bar drops with no read fan-out, at the price of lag and possibly lost increments.","section":"hot-spots"},{"id":"ch6/secondary-index","title":"Local vs global secondary indexes","caption":"Try <em>Find red cars</em> in Local mode, then turn on <em>P2 is slow</em> and repeat: the whole query waits for P2. Switch to Global and the same query asks one index partition. With Async updates, press <em>Add, then find at once</em>: the new car is missing. Drag <em>Index lag</em> to 0.9 s or less and press it again to see the race go the other way. Then press <em>Repaint a car</em>: the first repaint, #388 from red to silver, touches three partitions. The lag slider is slowed-down animation time; the latency stats are simulated milliseconds.","section":"secondary-indexes"},{"id":"ch6/rebalance-moves","title":"Add a node: what moves?","caption":"Try <em>+ Add node</em> under hash mod N, then switch to Fixed partitions and Ring + tokens and add a node again. Notice the red dots under mod N: keys shuffled between old nodes for no reason. Finally press Reset (back to 4 nodes), choose Ring + tokens with 1 token per node, and see one node own more than half the ring while another owns about 2%; raise the tokens and the shares even out.","section":"rebalancing"},{"id":"ch6/dynamic-splitting","title":"Dynamic splitting and merging","caption":"Try <em>Start writes</em> and watch P0 fill up and split; then turn on Pre-split, switch Keys to Sequential (timestamps), and finally press <em>Delete two-thirds of rows</em>. Notice that with sequential keys exactly one partition on one node takes the writes, however many splits happen. For clarity the viz moves each new upper half at once; HBase first keeps both halves on the parent's server and lets its balancer move one later.","section":"rebalancing-strategies"},{"id":"ch6/request-routing","title":"Routing through a partition move","caption":"Try Play in each Routing mode, then switch <em>Old owner checks ownership</em> off and play again. Notice where the stale map is caught in each mode (a MOVED redirect, a stale-version rejection, an extra forward), and that without the check node B acknowledges a write that the final read never sees.","section":"request-routing"}],"quizzes":9,"words":16130,"minutes":73},"ch7":{"sections":[{"id":"why-transactions","title":"Why transactions exist: two outcomes instead of a thousand"},{"id":"acid-unpacked","title":"ACID, letter by letter: what the promise really covers"},{"id":"aborts-and-retries","title":"Aborts and retries: when \"try again\" is safe"},{"id":"read-committed","title":"Read committed: never see or overwrite uncommitted data"},{"id":"snapshot-isolation","title":"Snapshot isolation: a frozen view built from row versions"},{"id":"lost-updates","title":"Lost updates: the read-modify-write trap"},{"id":"write-skew-phantoms","title":"Write skew and phantoms: two correct transactions, one broken invariant"},{"id":"serial-execution","title":"Actual serial execution: serializable by doing one thing at a time"},{"id":"two-phase-locking","title":"Two-phase locking: readers and writers block each other"},{"id":"ssi","title":"Serializable snapshot isolation: optimism that checks its premises"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch7/atomic-transfer","title":"All-or-nothing transfer","caption":"Try the default first: with no transaction, a crash after the debit makes $100 vanish. Then follow the Try next buttons. Transaction mode rolls the half-done transfer back, Retry completes it, a crash before the reply makes the retry move the money twice, and a transfer id turns that retry into a no-op. Notice the last stop: a transfer id without a transaction still leaves the accounts $100 short.","section":"aborts-and-retries"},{"id":"ch7/mvcc-snapshots","title":"MVCC snapshots","caption":"Try Play under Read committed first: T12's total comes out at $900. Switch to Snapshot isolation and replay: $1,000, because T13's versions are newer than T12's snapshot. Then pick Read uncommitted and set \"T13 ends with\" to Abort: the phone app shows $400, a balance that never existed. Notice that \"Writer waited for a reader?\" comes out no in every run, even though T13 overwrites a row T12 has already read: T12's read took no lock.","section":"snapshot-isolation"},{"id":"ch7/lost-update-race","title":"Lost update race: you pick the interleaving","caption":"Try Naive first: step Alice all the way to commit before Bob reads (44, nothing lost), then press Play the classic race, which interleaves the two reads (43). Then pick each fix and try to lose a like, and finish with the 4-client stress test. Notice that no schedule breaks a fix, and that under contention each one costs waits or retries.","section":"lost-updates"},{"id":"ch7/write-skew","title":"Write skew: doctors and double bookings","caption":"Try On-call doctors with No locks, then FOR UPDATE. Then switch to Room booking and play FOR UPDATE, Slot rows and EXCLUDE in turn. Notice that FOR UPDATE rescues the doctors but not the booking: when the check finds no rows, there is nothing to lock.","section":"write-skew-phantoms"},{"id":"ch7/two-phase-locking","title":"Two-phase locking and the phantom booking","caption":"Press Play on the default, Index-range locks: both shared locks coexist, the inserts deadlock, a victim is aborted and its retry sees Alice's booking. Then follow the Try next buttons: Row locks only lets the phantom through, range locks let Bob book Room 124 in parallel, and No index locks the whole table. Notice the false conflict there: bookings for two different rooms deadlock.","section":"two-phase-locking"},{"id":"ch7/ssi-detector","title":"SSI: catching stale premises","caption":"Try Write after read under Snapshot isolation, then under SSI, and watch where the abort happens. Then step through Stale read, Read-only reader and Writer aborts. Finally, in You schedule it, try to get both doctors off call under SSI. Notice that nobody ever waits, and only a read-write transaction whose premise was overwritten by a committed transaction gets aborted.","section":"ssi"}],"quizzes":10,"words":16950,"minutes":77},"ch8":{"sections":[{"id":"partial-failure","title":"Partial failure: the problem one computer never had"},{"id":"unreliable-networks","title":"A missing reply tells you almost nothing"},{"id":"timeouts-and-delays","title":"Every timeout is a bet"},{"id":"two-clocks","title":"Two clocks, two different questions"},{"id":"ordering-by-timestamps","title":"Timestamps lie unless you know the error bars"},{"id":"process-pauses","title":"The node that froze mid-sentence"},{"id":"truth-by-majority","title":"A node cannot trust its own judgment"},{"id":"fencing-tokens","title":"Leases, locks and fencing tokens"},{"id":"byzantine-faults","title":"When nodes lie: Byzantine faults"},{"id":"system-models","title":"System models: writing down what can go wrong"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch8/no-reply","title":"What happened to my request?","caption":"Try Mystery mode first: press Send, watch only the client side, and guess whether the customer was charged before the truth is revealed. Then pick Reply lost, Send, and press Retry once the timeout fires. Notice the charge counter reaches 2. Finally turn on \"Server remembers request IDs\" and repeat with Request queued: the duplicate is answered from the saved result, and the customer is charged once whatever the timing.","section":"unreliable-networks"},{"id":"ch8/failure-detector","title":"Tuning a failure detector","caption":"A simplified φ accrual model. Try raising Network load to 90% with a 1.2 s timeout and press Run: each red band is a moment when M declared a live B dead. Drag the timeout up until the false alarms stop, then press Crash B and read off how long detection now takes. Notice that no fixed timeout gives both zero false alarms and fast detection. Switch to Adaptive (φ) and watch the threshold climb as load rises, and keep climbing with frequent GC pauses.","section":"timeouts-and-delays"},{"id":"ch8/clock-skew-lww","title":"Skewed clocks eat writes","caption":"Try the Book example first: Node 1's clock is 3 ms ahead, so A's earlier write carries the larger timestamp, and B's increment is discarded on every replica even though B got an acknowledgment. Drag Node 1's clock back to 0 and x = 2 survives; set it to +2 ms and the timestamps tie, so the winner is decided by the node-ID tiebreaker rather than by what happened first. Then pick Lagging writer and notice that B's retries are thrown away until Node 3's clock catches up with A's timestamp.","section":"ordering-by-timestamps"},{"id":"ch8/commit-wait","title":"TrueTime and commit wait","caption":"Try the opening state: with commit wait off and worst-case clock errors, T2 starts after T1 is visible yet gets the smaller timestamp, so its snapshot misses T1. Turn on Commit wait and the order is fixed, at a visible cost of 2ε of latency; drag ε to watch the price grow. Then pick Broken clock, where both clocks are off by more than the ε they promise: even with commit wait on, T2 gets the smaller timestamp again, unless you drag the gap long enough to cover the error by luck.","section":"ordering-by-timestamps"},{"id":"ch8/majority-partition","title":"Who gets to decide?","caption":"Try Clean split and press Client writes to every island: only the side with 3 of 5 votes accepts. Then turn off Require a majority, write again, and Heal network. Notice that with the rule on, at most one island can ever decide; with it off you get split brain, and healing throws away acknowledged writes. Also try the two datacenter presets and Even split, which uses 4 nodes. The Nodes control replays the current scenario at another cluster size.","section":"truth-by-majority"},{"id":"ch8/fencing-token","title":"Fencing off a zombie leaseholder","caption":"Try pressing Play with fencing off and watch step 8 corrupt the file; then switch on Storage checks fencing tokens and replay. Then set Why C1 is late to Slow network and drag the delay slider. Notice that the outcome depends only on whether storage checks the token, never on how long C1 was late or why.","section":"fencing-tokens"}],"quizzes":10,"words":18718,"minutes":85},"ch9":{"sections":[{"id":"linearizability","title":"Linearizability: making many copies behave like one"},{"id":"linearizability-in-practice","title":"Who needs one copy, and which replication schemes can provide it"},{"id":"cost-of-linearizability","title":"The price of one copy: CAP and the latency floor"},{"id":"causality","title":"Causality: the order that actually matters"},{"id":"lamport-timestamps","title":"Lamport timestamps: a total order without clocks, and its blind spot"},{"id":"total-order-broadcast","title":"Total order broadcast: if everyone reads the same log, everyone agrees"},{"id":"two-phase-commit","title":"Two-phase commit: a protocol of promises"},{"id":"distributed-transactions-in-practice","title":"Why distributed transactions got a bad name"},{"id":"consensus","title":"Consensus: agreeing despite failures, with epochs and majorities"},{"id":"coordination-services","title":"ZooKeeper and etcd: consensus you don't have to write"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch9/linearizability-checker","title":"Is this history linearizable?","caption":"Try the opening scene first: in Followers mode with 2.5 s of lag, Bob's read starts after Alice's has finished, yet returns the older value. Drag the lag to 1.7 s or less and the violation disappears, but only because F2 happened to catch up in time. Then switch to Leader (always linearizable) and to Quorum. Notice that w + r &gt; n still lets Bob go back in time, until you turn on Sync read repair and Alice's read pays an extra round trip.","section":"linearizability"},{"id":"ch9/cap-partition","title":"When the link breaks: consistency or availability","caption":"Try cutting the link in Linearizable mode, then buying and checking stock from West: both are refused while East keeps selling. Switch to Always available, repeat, then heal the link. Notice the oversold count, and that in Linearizable mode every West purchase pays the full round trip even while the link is healthy. Then, in Always available mode with the link up and one ticket left, press Both buy at once: two leaders can oversell without any partition.","section":"cost-of-linearizability"},{"id":"ch9/logical-clocks","title":"Lamport clocks vs vector clocks","caption":"Try tapping one event to light up its causal past (green) and future (blue), then tap two events to compare their clocks; the opening scene preselects two concurrent events where L(A) &lt; L(B). Notice the <span class=\"mono\">||</span> marks in the total-order strip: those neighbors are concurrent, so their order is an accident of counter values and tie-breaks, not causality. Add your own local events and messages with the Action menu, then load Username race to see why the order isn't final until you've heard from everyone.","section":"lamport-timestamps"},{"id":"ch9/total-order-broadcast","title":"Same log, same state","caption":"Try racing Ana and Ben for a few names in Plain broadcast mode with high jitter, then switch to Total order broadcast and race again. Notice that plain broadcast can leave replicas disagreeing and tell both clients yes, while the sequenced log keeps every replica identical and holds back any number that arrives early. Then, in Total order broadcast mode, have Ben check a name mid-race, first via his local replica and then via the log. The Log leader stands in for a consensus group: in a real system it is a Raft or Zab leader that gets each entry onto a majority before it is delivered.","section":"total-order-broadcast"},{"id":"ch9/two-phase-commit","title":"Two-phase commit and the in-doubt window","caption":"Try one clean run, then set the coordinator to crash after logging the decision, mid-send, and run again. Notice that Orders commits while Payments and Inventory sit in doubt with their lock timers climbing; press tx99 updates acct#7 and it queues behind Payments' lock until you recover the coordinator. Then repeat that crash, press the admin's heuristic abort before recovering, and watch atomicity break. A crash before the decision is logged ends in presumed abort instead.","section":"two-phase-commit"},{"id":"ch9/raft-consensus","title":"Raft: terms, votes and majorities","caption":"Try Isolate leader, wait until the other four elect a new leader, press Client write a couple of times (each side's client writes to its own leader), then set the network back to Healthy. Notice that the old leader's new entries stay amber (uncommitted) while the majority side's leader, in a higher term, keeps committing, and that on healing the stale entries flash red and are replaced. Then tap three servers to crash them and watch progress stop; tap them again to restart. Simplifications: followers copy the leader's whole log instead of Raft's prefix matching, and there's no PreVote or CheckQuorum, so an isolated leader believes it leads until it hears a higher term (etcd enables both, so leaders step down sooner and rejoining nodes don't force needless elections).","section":"consensus"}],"quizzes":10,"words":17896,"minutes":81},"ch10":{"sections":[{"id":"why-batch","title":"Batch jobs trade latency for throughput"},{"id":"unix-pipelines","title":"The Unix pipeline: the first batch framework"},{"id":"mapreduce-and-hdfs","title":"MapReduce: a Unix pipeline spread across a thousand machines"},{"id":"reduce-side-joins","title":"Reduce-side joins: let the shuffle bring related records together"},{"id":"map-side-joins","title":"Map-side joins: skip the shuffle when you know your data's shape"},{"id":"batch-outputs","title":"Batch outputs: build it offline, swap it in whole"},{"id":"hadoop-vs-mpp","title":"Why Hadoop spread alongside MPP databases (and why it loves disks)"},{"id":"dataflow-engines","title":"Beyond MapReduce: stop writing every step to disk"},{"id":"graph-processing","title":"Graphs and iteration: think like a vertex"},{"id":"high-level-apis","title":"High-level APIs: declarative where it helps, code where it must"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch10/mapreduce-shuffle","title":"Inside a MapReduce job","caption":"Press Play to push a 12-line access log through every stage, then switch Reducers to 3, turn on the Combiner, and try both Failure modes. Notice that the final counts never change (/home 6, /docs 3, /cart 2, /blog 1); only the number of records shuffled and the busiest reducer's load do. 'Node B dies after map' is the subtle failure: a task that had already finished runs again, because its output lived only on its own local disk.","section":"mapreduce-and-hdfs"},{"id":"ch10/stragglers-skew","title":"Stragglers and hot keys","caption":"Start at the default 35% hot-key share and turn on Speculative execution: the stage gets no shorter, because R3's backup has the same 3.45 M records to process. Then set the share to 0% and turn on Slow machine: now the backup wins. Finally turn Slow machine off, set the share back to 35%, turn on Split hot key and vary k; then drop the share to 0% with the split still on. Notice that the idle area after each finished bar, not the busy bars, is what a skewed stage wastes, and that splitting a key that isn't hot is pure overhead.","section":"reduce-side-joins"},{"id":"ch10/join-strategies","title":"Picking a join strategy","caption":"Try Broadcast with the default 1 GB users table and 4 GB per task, then drag Users table up to 10 GB. Next set Input layout to Bucketed + sorted and push the users table to 200 GB: Partitioned runs out of memory while Merge carries on. Finally choose 'Bucketed, different hash' and press 'Let the optimizer pick'. Notice the two very different ways a shortcut fails: loudly (out of memory, tasks killed) or silently (wrong answers).","section":"map-side-joins"},{"id":"ch10/batch-output","title":"Publishing batch output","caption":"Try the default first, Writing rows into the live DB with the task failure on, and press Play. Notice that half the users see v42 while the job is still running, the web app's p99 jumps from 12 to 85 ms, and T3's retry leaves 30,000 duplicate rows. Then switch to Building files, then swapping and replay: readers see nothing until one atomic switch, and the failed attempt leaves no trace. Finally turn on The new code has a bug in both modes and compare how long the undo takes (hours versus seconds). All numbers are illustrative.","section":"batch-outputs"},{"id":"ch10/materialize-vs-pipeline","title":"Materialize or pipeline?","caption":"Try the defaults first: compare the finish times (44 vs 26 minutes) and the GB written to HDFS ×3 stat (MR 603 vs DF 3). Switch the exchange to Stage by stage and notice the dataflow engine only slows to 29 minutes. Then turn on Machine W2 dies: MapReduce reruns 2 tasks from job 1's output in HDFS, while the stage-by-stage engine recomputes only W2's partition from the original input. Switch back to Pipelined: nothing was kept between operators, so all 12 tasks restart. Finally return to Stage by stage, turn on Count operator is nondeterministic, and watch three finished top-k tasks get discarded. All times are illustrative.","section":"dataflow-engines"},{"id":"ch10/pregel-pagerank","title":"PageRank, one superstep at a time","caption":"Try Step superstep a few times and watch ranks flow along the links: A, with five incoming links, grows, while H, with none, drops to the 0.15/8 baseline (0.019). Switch Partitioning to Link-aware and notice cross-worker messages fall from 11 to 5 per superstep while the final ranks stay identical. Then run with a checkpoint every 2 supersteps, crash worker 2, and compare how many supersteps are redone with checkpoints Off.","section":"graph-processing"}],"quizzes":10,"words":16753,"minutes":76},"ch11":{"sections":[{"id":"why-streams","title":"Why wait for tomorrow? From nightly batches to event streams"},{"id":"messaging-systems","title":"Messaging systems: what happens when consumers fall behind or crash"},{"id":"log-based-brokers","title":"Logs as brokers: why Kafka never deletes on read"},{"id":"change-data-capture","title":"Keeping systems in sync: dual writes fail, change data capture works"},{"id":"event-sourcing","title":"Event sourcing: record what happened, derive what is"},{"id":"state-and-immutability","title":"State is the sum of its changes: immutability, many views, and the right to be forgotten"},{"id":"processing-streams","title":"What stream processors do: alerts, analytics, views and search"},{"id":"reasoning-about-time","title":"Whose clock is it? Event time, stragglers and watermarks"},{"id":"stream-joins","title":"Joining streams: three kinds of join, and why time makes them nondeterministic"},{"id":"fault-tolerance","title":"Exactly-once, really: checkpoints, atomic commits and idempotence"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch11/queue-vs-log","title":"Queue vs log: what happens after a read","caption":"Try Crash consumer within the first second: C1 dies holding m3, the broker redelivers it after the ack timeout, and m3 completes after m4, marked red. Then press Add audit subscriber and Replay analytics. Notice that the queue has nothing to give a new subscriber, because acknowledged messages were deleted, while on the right the log replays its whole history, and a crashed consumer re-reads messages in order instead of reordering them.","section":"messaging-systems"},{"id":"ch11/partitioned-log","title":"Partitions and consumer groups","caption":"Press Run, then drag Consumers to 6: the topic has only four partitions, so two consumers get none and sit idle. Try Slow message, then Hot key A, then Add partition and watch which keys move. Finally set Consumers to 1, Produce rate to 12 and Retention to 8. Notice that each problem stays inside one partition or one consumer, until lag outruns retention: then unread messages are deleted and silently skipped.","section":"log-based-brokers"},{"id":"ch11/dual-writes","title":"Dual writes vs change data capture","caption":"Step through Dual writes with each fault (Race, Write fails, Crash, then Nothing), pressing Play to watch the timeline unfold. Then switch Strategy to CDC and replay the same faults. Notice that dual writes look perfect on the happy path, which is exactly why they pass testing, while CDC always converges to X = B, paying with a short window in which the index is stale.","section":"change-data-capture"},{"id":"ch11/event-time-windows","title":"Event time, watermarks and late events","caption":"Try switching Count by to Processing time and comparing each window with its true count: delayed orders land in the minute they arrived, not the minute they happened. Back in event time, drag Watermark delay from 20 s to 100 s and notice the dropped stragglers disappear while Avg result delay climbs past 100 s. Press Phone was offline: no sensible delay saves those orders, so switch Late events to Update result and watch windows turn blue, correct but emitted more than once. Set the delay to 0 to see even ordinary orders arrive late, and press Play to watch the watermark advance and windows fire.","section":"reasoning-about-time"},{"id":"ch11/stream-joins","title":"Stream joins: windows, tables and time","caption":"Try dragging Window from 8 up to 10 minutes and watch s3 flip from an orphan click to a click-through: the window defines what counts. Back at 8, turn on Searches arrive 3 min late and turn off Buffer clicks too: the early clicks find no search and are thrown away, and CTR falls from 50% to 0%. Switch to Enrichment: sale #2 happened on Jun 30 but was uploaded after the rate change, so it gets 16%. Press Replay in January and notice that the same four sales produce different invoices. Finally choose Rate at sale time, and notice the price in Rate versions kept: the changelog can no longer be compacted.","section":"stream-joins"},{"id":"ch11/exactly-once","title":"Crash, restart, recount: delivery guarantees","caption":"Try At-least-once and press Crash at worst moment: e4 and e5 are written, the crash lands before the next commit, and the replay adds them again ($255). Set Commit every to 1 and only e5 is doubled. Then try At-most-once (e5 is lost, $200), Atomic commit and Idempotent sink (both $215). Finally turn on Email per order and notice that only the sink that passes an idempotency key to the email service avoids duplicate emails: a transaction inside the pipeline can't take back an email.","section":"fault-tolerance"}],"quizzes":10,"words":19289,"minutes":88},"ch12":{"sections":[{"id":"data-integration","title":"No database does everything: integrate by deriving data"},{"id":"reprocessing","title":"Reprocessing history: from Lambda to Kappa"},{"id":"unbundling-databases","title":"The database, turned inside out"},{"id":"write-path-read-path","title":"Where the write path meets the read path"},{"id":"end-to-end-argument","title":"Exactly once, end to end: only the endpoints can deduplicate"},{"id":"enforcing-constraints","title":"Constraints without distributed transactions"},{"id":"timeliness-and-integrity","title":"Late is fixable, wrong is forever: timeliness vs integrity"},{"id":"trust-but-verify","title":"Trust, but verify: systems that check themselves"},{"id":"predictive-analytics","title":"When the model says no: bias, accountability and feedback loops"},{"id":"privacy-and-tracking","title":"Data is about people: surveillance, consent and keeping less"}],"viz":[{"id":"ch12/unfriend-race","title":"The unfriend race: one log vs two","caption":"Try 'Two logs' with high lag and press Play: the post reaches the Notifier at 0.7 s, before the unfriend, and Ben is notified. Switch to 'One log': lag delays both events but can never reorder them. Then go back to two logs and pick 'Carry version seen': the post is held for 1.3 s until the friend list catches up. Notice that the fix costs timeliness, not correctness. Toy timings.","section":"data-integration"},{"id":"ch12/lambda-vs-kappa","title":"Fixing history: Lambda vs Kappa","caption":"Try Lambda with 'Speed layer only' and step through midnight: today's plays drop from 600 to 420, then jump back to 600 when they move into the batch view. Then try Kappa with 'Forever' and with '7 days'. Notice that Kappa's fix lives in one codebase, but a replay can only rebuild what the log still holds.","section":"reprocessing"},{"id":"ch12/write-read-boundary","title":"Moving the write/read boundary","caption":"Try the two presets: with 100k reads/s and 1 write/s, precomputing every query becomes cheapest; with 1,000 writes/s and almost no reads, doing nothing wins. With 'Index + cache' selected, drag the cache hit rate. Notice that the two kinds of control do different jobs: picking a strategy (or clicking its bar) slides the dashed boundary along the pipeline and highlights that bar, while the workload sliders resize every bar. Each bar is the same search job, split differently between write-path and read-path work.","section":"write-path-read-path"},{"id":"ch12/end-to-end-dedup","title":"Who catches the duplicate?","caption":"Try each fault with 'Per-layer only', then with 'End-to-end ID'. Notice where each retry starts: a double-click starts above TCP, a lost reply starts a new transaction, and a consumer crash replays into a database outside Kafka's bracket. Only the request ID minted in the browser spans the whole path, so it is the only protection that catches all three.","section":"end-to-end-argument"},{"id":"ch12/stream-transfer","title":"A transfer without 2PC","caption":"Try the default 'Splitter crash' with deduplication on, then the same crash with it off, then 'Redelivery to P2' both ways. Notice that with deduplication every run returns to $1,000 and the $50 'in flight' only delays the credit, while without it the books end at $950 or $1,050 with nothing in flight: no amount of waiting fixes that. 'Cross-partition commits' stays at 0 throughout.","section":"enforcing-constraints"},{"id":"ch12/feedback-loop","title":"The self-fulfilling model","caption":"Try 'Run 10 rounds' twice with the defaults, then Reset and repeat with 'Explore' on, or with the starting gap at 0. Notice that the approval gap persists although both groups start with identical ability, that G2's approved borrowers repay at least as often as G1's (so the lender's own data looks fine), and how many creditworthy G2 applicants end up pushed below the bar.","section":"predictive-analytics"}],"quizzes":10,"words":20320,"minutes":92}};
